Subject: The unit binds a class once: its shape from the prototype and the field list, objects made in it, globals read without code

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_class_shape(ctx, proto, atoms, n)`: the hashed shape an object with prototype `proto` reaches by adding the fields in order (a counted reference). Every transition looks a hashed shape up first and the reference keeps it in the table, so the engine's own constructs of the class end on the same address. The walk's private object is exempt from slot-claim checks (`js_aot_shape_scratch`): its undefined placeholders are no evidence against a claim.
  - `qjs_aot_new_from_shape(ctx, shape)`: a new ordinary object of that shape, fields undefined; `qjs_aot_object_filled(ctx, obj)` runs the slot-claim check of a contracted layout once the caller has written the fields.
  - `qjs_aot_ctor_prepare(ctx, fn)`: the engine's once-per-constructor work before a first construct (the unit's layout chain), for a region that makes the class's objects itself.
  - `qjs_aot_global_get(ctx, atom, &v)`: a global binding's value without running code (a lexical binding first, then an own data property of the global object), borrowed; 0 for an accessor, a missing binding or one in its dead zone.

Why we need it
  The region compiler (TRUST) binds every proven class at its init instead of learning shapes from receivers at run time: dispatch is a compare with the class shapes, a construct makes the object in its final shape and fills fixed slots, a receiver that is a global (`planner`) is loaded from its binding.

Soundness
  No JS runs in any of them. The class shape is an ordinary hashed shape; an object made in it is indistinguishable from one the engine built field by field (the region checks the claims at the end). A global read leaves the compiled code for anything but a plain data binding.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
